\details{
Decoding is greedy when \code{temperature = 0} — the exact, reproducible path — and
temperature + nucleus (top-p) sampling otherwise. Sampling is drawn on the CPU
from a seeded generator, so a run is fully reproducible: the same \code{seed} and
arguments produce the same text across runs and sessions. When \code{seed = NULL}
a seed is drawn (from R's RNG, so \code{set.seed()} makes even that reproducible)
and \strong{recorded} — the seed actually used is always returned as
\code{attr(result, "seed")}, so any generation can be replayed.

Without a schema, generation stops at \code{max_tokens}, at the model's
end-of-generation token, or
as soon as one of the \code{stop} strings appears (the output is truncated just
before it). A prompt longer than the model's context window raises
\code{relm_error_context_overflow}, whose message states by how much.

With the supported Spark-X2.5 chat template, ordinary chat opens the response
with the author's \verb{<think>} marker. Generated reasoning text and closing
markers are returned as generated and count toward \code{max_tokens}. With a
\code{schema}, chat instead uses the author's \verb{</think>} non-thinking opener, so
the JSON constraint starts at the first generated token. This does not strip
or repair model output. Spark chat supports the pinned official template and
the existing single user turn; custom templates require \code{chat = FALSE} and
caller-supplied formatting. Other models keep their existing chat behavior.
